{
  "module": "Inference: prefill, decode and the cache",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "context-window-limits",
    "title": "What limits the context window",
    "summary": "The three things that cap how long a text a model can handle: the trained position range, the n² attention cost, and a KV cache that grows with n, and what can be done about it.",
    "covers": ["context-window-limits"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "sequence-shapes",
        "reason": "The context window is a limit on the sequence length n."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "absolute-positions",
        "reason": "A learned position table has no rows beyond its trained length."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "scaling-and-cost",
        "reason": "Attention cost grows with the square of n."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "kv-cache-size",
        "reason": "The cache grows linearly with n."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "three-limits",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A model has a "
            },
            {
              "text": "context window",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": a limit on how many tokens it can take in at once. What sets that limit? Three separate things, and any of them can be the one that binds:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "the "
              },
              {
                "text": "position scheme's trained range",
                "bold": true
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "the "
              },
              {
                "text": "O(n²) attention cost",
                "bold": true
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "a "
              },
              {
                "text": "KV cache that grows linearly",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " with n"
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 85)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "position-range",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Position range.",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " A model is trained on texts up to some length. With learned absolute position embeddings there is simply "
            },
            {
              "text": "no row",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " for a position beyond n_ctx, so the model has no position vector to give a longer text."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Other schemes have their own trained range: the model has only learned to behave well over the positions it saw."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 85, 47)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "attention-cost",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Attention cost.",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " The score matrix has n² entries per head. At n = 4,000 that is 16,000,000 scores, and at n = 8,000 it is 64,000,000: doubling the text makes attention four times as expensive."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 85, 37)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "cache-growth",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Cache growth.",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " The KV cache grows linearly with n. For Llama-2-7B at 0.5 MiB per token, that is 2 GiB at 4,096 tokens and 16 GiB at 32,768, for a single sequence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Unlike the other two, this is a plain matter of memory: a long enough text simply does not fit."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 85, 78)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "extending",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Techniques such as "
            },
            {
              "text": "position interpolation",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " or "
            },
            {
              "text": "RoPE scaling",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " can extend a trained model's usable context."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "How well they work depends on the method and on further fine-tuning. Stretching the position range does nothing about the other two limits: the attention cost and the cache still grow with n."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 85)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
